  "generated_text": "1. **Identify the conditions for the roots to be real:**\n   - The polynomial $x^4 + 2ax^3 + (2a - 2)x^2 + (-4a + 3)x - 2$ will have all real roots if and only if its discriminant $\\Delta$ is non-negative.\n\n2. **Calculate the discriminant:**\n   - The discriminant $\\Delta$ of the polynomial is given by a complex expression involving $a$. However, without loss of generality, we can use the fact that the polynomial has all real roots if and only if $\\Delta \\geq 0$.\n\n3. **Determine the range of $a$ for which $\\Delta \\geq 0$:**\n   - Solve the inequality for $\\Delta \\geq 0$ to find the range of $a$ for which the polynomial has all real roots.\n\n4. **Calculate the probability:**\n   - The probability that the polynomial has all real roots is the ratio of the length of the interval where $\\Delta \\geq 0$ to the total length of the interval $[-20, 18]$.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - Express the probability as a fraction.",
  "grade": {
